  "query_preview": "..\n\nInfluence of high pressure is waning this afternoon as amplifying \ncentral CONUS ridging builds across the Midwest. This mid-upper \npattern shift allows a ridge running wave train to drop into the \ncentral Great Lakes through the remainder of the weekend and Monday \noffering multiple chances for storms locally. \n\nExpansive coverage of stratus crossing Lake Michigan demarcates the \nlead edge of the first of these shortwaves which is set to cross SE \nMI this evening. Dry resident airmass in place (12Z KDTX RAOB showed \n0.72\" of PW) precludes any rain chances with the main consequence \ninstead being the initiation of moisture advection into southern \nlower MI through tonight. Lower level moisture is established by \nSunday morning tied to the arrival a second wave offering the \nchances for scattered showers and isolated thunder first over the \nSaginaw Valley. Chances then shift south of the course of the \nmorning as the shortwave and the left exit region of an upper \nMidwestern jet streak shift southeast. Some afternoon-evening \nredevelopment remains possible from peak heating though better \nconvective signal comes late evening-Sunday night when northern MI \nconvection drops south into/near the area. Any storms that do manage \nto develop in the evening stand the best shot at becoming strong to \nsevere with ~1000J/kg of MLCAPE still available and shear 35-40kts. \nWhile instability partially wanes by time of any northern MI \nconvection reaches the area, shear holds unchanged maintaining \norganization and isolated severe potential. Primary hazards would be \ndamaging wind gusts and locally heavy rainfall with hail being a \nlesser secondary threat. All of SE MI is under a Marginal Risk from \nSPC.\n\nModel signal early Monday still muddled with mid-range solutions \nhighly varied in the degree of lingering morning showers/storms \nwhich affects the magnitude of available instability late day. That \nsaid, confidence is still higher, relative to prior waves, for \nMonday evening/night in organized severe potential as a mid-level \ntrough digs into the northern Great Lakes. In addition to the \nstronger synoptic kinematics, attendant surface cold front dropping \nthrough the state also provides more robust forcing mechanism \nresulting in good model agreement for MCS development. Its exact \nplacement/track however is the main point of uncertainty with a wide \nrange of outcomes currently advertised in the model suite ranging \nfrom west MI to eastern Lake Huron. Given the convective mode, \nsevere wind gusts are the primary hazard with a low but non-zero \nchance for a QLCS tornado. All of SE MI remains under a Day 3 Slight \nRisk from SPC. \n\nLow pressure peels east of the region by Tuesday leaving cooler, \ndrier airmass in its place as 850mb temps fall to lower teens C with \nsub 60F dewpoints. Resultant midweek highs hold slightly below \naverage in the lower 80s including some upper 70s possible \nWednesday. Surface high pressure sinking out of northern Ontario \npromotes clearer skies supporting another period of pleasant \nweather.",
